C = [
("h1", "5. The Curse"),
("p", "Claim: Galatians 3:10–14 says that Christ bore the curse of the law, and it says it inside an argument about the law and the promise. The curse is the curse the law pronounces on those who are under it. Christ was “made a curse for us.” The passage does not say that every person stood under that curse, and the volume does not say it. What the passage does say is large enough, and the volume reads it as written."),
("sub", "5.1 The place of the passage in the letter"),
("p", "Paul has told the Galatians that he is amazed that they are turning to another gospel (Galatians 1:6–9), and he has said of Christ that he “gave himself for our sins, that he might deliver us from this present evil world, according to the will of God and our Father” (Galatians 1:4). In chapter 3 he turns on them: “O foolish Galatians, who hath bewitched you, that ye should not obey the truth, before whose eyes Jesus Christ hath been evidently set forth, crucified among you?” (Galatians 3:1). The Cross is the thing set before their eyes. The question of the chapter is whether the Spirit and the blessing come by the works of the law or by the hearing of faith."),
("sub", "5.2 Verses 10 to 14 clause by clause"),
("ctbl", "What Galatians 3:10–14 says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“as many as are of the works of the law are under the curse”", "Those who are of the works of the law are under a curse", "That any are under it who are not of the works of the law; the clause is bounded by “as many as”"),
    ("“Cursed is every one that continueth not in all things which are written in the book of the law to do them”", "The curse is pronounced on whoever does not continue in all that is written", "Whether anyone does continue; Paul draws the conclusion in verse 11"),
    ("“no man is justified by the law in the sight of God”", "Justification is not by the law", "Nothing about who is under the law; the sentence is a statement about justification"),
    ("“Christ hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us”", "Christ redeemed “us” from the curse of the law, by being made a curse for us", "Who “us” includes beyond Paul and those he writes as “us”; the clause does not define it"),
    ("“Cursed is every one that hangeth on a tree”", "The Scripture Paul cites for the curse on one who hangs on a tree", "That the Cross is the only form of the curse; the verse cites the tree"),
    ("“That the blessing of Abraham might come on the Gentiles through Jesus Christ”", "A purpose: the blessing of Abraham on the Gentiles through Jesus Christ", "How the blessing reaches them in detail; the Abrahamic covenant is not the subject of this volume"),
    ("“that we might receive the promise of the Spirit through faith”", "A second purpose: that we receive the promise of the Spirit through faith", "Whether the two purposes are one"),
  ], [0.33, 0.33, 0.34]),
("sub", "5.3 What “the curse of the law” is"),
("p", "The phrase is not a general name for the penalty of sin. It is a phrase with a text behind it, which Paul quotes in verse 10. The law pronounces a curse: “Cursed is every one that continueth not in all things which are written in the book of the law to do them.” The curse falls on the one who does not continue. The words of Moses that Paul’s sentence echoes are these: “Cursed be he that confirmeth not all the words of this law to do them” (Deuteronomy 27:26). The curse of the law is the curse the law itself pronounces, and it falls on those who are under it and who do not do it. That is how Paul uses the phrase, and Thomas Constable reads it in the same way, calling it “the curse pronounced by the Law on the law-breaker.” The volume follows him there."),
("p", "Paul then cites a second text for the form of the curse: “Cursed is every one that hangeth on a tree.” The words are from Deuteronomy 21:23, where the man who is put to death and hanged on a tree is “accursed of God,” and his body is not to remain all night on the tree. Paul does not say in Galatians 3:13 why this second text applies to the Cross beyond saying that it is written. The pairing is Paul’s. Christ was made a curse, and it is written that every one who hangs on a tree is cursed. The volume reports the pairing and does not add to it."),
("sub", "5.4 “Us”"),
("p", "“Christ hath redeemed us from the curse of the law” (Galatians 3:13). The word “us” is the word Paul uses, and the volume keeps it. Paul writes to the churches of Galatia. The passage does not say that the “us” of verse 13 is every person who has ever lived, and it does not say that the “us” had been under the curse in the same way as the Gentiles of verse 14, who are named separately. The “us” of verse 13 and the “Gentiles” of verse 14 are both in the passage, and the passage presents the redemption of the first as the means to the blessing of the second."),
("p", "Thomas Constable reads the verse more widely. He writes that “Christ paid the penalty for our sins and made justification possible for every person,” and that “He voluntarily took the wrath of God that was directed toward us upon Himself.” The volume does not dispute that Christ bore the curse. It disputes that the verse says more than it says about the extent of those who stood under the curse of the law. The extent of those redeemed is given in the clause “redeemed us” and the purposes in verse 14. The objections chapter returns to the point."),
("sub", "5.5 The Cross in Galatians 2:20, 5:11 and 6:14"),
("p", "The curse is not the only way the letter speaks of the Cross. Paul writes, “I am crucified with Christ: nevertheless I live; yet not I, but Christ liveth in me: and the life which I now live in the flesh I live by the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and gave himself for me” (Galatians 2:20). He adds, “I do not frustrate the grace of God: for if righteousness come by the law, then Christ is dead in vain” (Galatians 2:21). He says of the preaching of circumcision that “then is the offence of the cross ceased” (Galatians 5:11), and he closes: “But God forbid that I should glory, save in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, by whom the world is crucified unto me, and I unto the world” (Galatians 6:14)."),
("ctbl", "The Cross in four verses of Galatians.", [
    ("Verse", "What the Cross is said to do", "Against what"),
    ("Galatians 2:20", "Paul is crucified with Christ; Christ loved him and gave himself for him", "A life lived by the flesh; the passage is personal"),
    ("Galatians 2:21", "Christ’s death would be in vain if righteousness came by the law", "Righteousness by the law"),
    ("Galatians 5:11", "The offence of the cross would cease if circumcision were preached", "Circumcision and persecution"),
    ("Galatians 6:14", "The world is crucified to Paul and Paul to the world", "Glorying in the flesh; the preceding verses name circumcision"),
  ], [0.20, 0.50, 0.30]),
("p", "The curse of Galatians 3:13 is one of these. It is the one with a quotation behind it. The others speak of the Cross as the thing that makes the law an inadequate ground (2:21), that offends (5:11), and that separates Paul from the world (6:14). None of them is the curse again."),
("sub", "5.6 The seed, the blessing and the Body"),
("p", "Galatians 3 is also the chapter in which the seed, the promises to Abraham and the inheritance are argued, and the volume does not take up those subjects. They belong to the volumes of the Covenantal Commentary Series, and in particular to Abraham’s Seed. One sentence governs the volume’s use of the chapter: the seed is prophetic fulfillment in Christ; the Body receives blessing through Christ alone, not through participation in the Abrahamic covenant. The volume therefore reads Galatians 3:13 as a statement of what Christ did about the curse of the law, and it does not read verse 14 as a statement that the Body is enrolled in a covenant with Abraham. What verse 14 says is that the blessing of Abraham comes on the Gentiles “through Jesus Christ,” and that the promise of the Spirit is received “through faith.”"),
]
